Meeting Summarizer: AI Tool that Converts Meeting Transcripts into Action Plans

Slide 1 — Title (20 seconds)
Good morning everyone. I'm K. Veeresh, presenting our paper titled "Meeting Summarizer: AI Tool that Converts Meeting Transcripts into Action Plans" from Vel Tech University, Chennai. This work is co-authored with E. Nandu Royal and our guide Dr. D. Dhinakaran.

Slide 2 — Outline (15 seconds)
I'll walk you through the motivation, our proposed system, the model architecture, evaluation results, and conclude with future directions. Let's get started.

Slide 3 — Introduction (45 seconds)
With the rapid shift to virtual and hybrid work environments, the number of professional meetings has increased significantly. Every meeting generates large volumes of unstructured conversational data — and the challenge is not recording them, it's making sense of them afterward.
Participants often struggle to recall who was assigned what, what decisions were made, and what the deadlines were. This leads to missed follow-ups, poor accountability, and reduced organizational efficiency.
Existing tools — whether ASR-based recorders or generic summarizers — only give you a wall of text. They don't tell you what to do next. That gap is exactly what we address.

Slide 4 — Problem Statement (40 seconds)
Let me formalize the problem. Current meeting tools fall short in three ways.
First, transcript-only systems produce verbatim text — but reading through a 30-minute transcript to find your two action items is inefficient and cognitively demanding.
Second, summarization tools produce narrative summaries — descriptive, not actionable. They tell you what was discussed, not what needs to be done.
Third, ML-based action extraction systems exist in research, but they operate in isolation — not integrated with speech processing — and lack multilingual support.
Our objective is simple: design one end-to-end system that takes raw audio or transcript as input and produces a structured, actionable plan with owners and deadlines as output.

Slide 5 — Related Work (30 seconds)
Prior work in this space falls into three categories. Transcript-based documentation systems improve accessibility but put the burden of interpretation on the user. Summarization approaches using extractive and abstractive transformer models — like BERT and T5 — produce readable summaries but are not action-oriented. And ML-based action extraction methods using dialogue act classification have shown promise but remain siloed pipelines.
Our work integrates all three into a single modular system.

Slide 6 — Proposed System Architecture (60 seconds)
Here is the overall architecture. The system has five key modules.
The Audio Processing module takes either a live stream or recorded file and converts it to text using an ASR engine with multilingual support and timestamping.
The Speaker Identification module assigns speaker labels to each utterance — so we always know who said what.
The NLP Pipeline normalizes text, segments sentences, performs named entity recognition, and categorizes each utterance as a discussion point, decision, question, or action proposal.
The Summarization Engine uses transformer-based models to generate concise, contextually accurate summaries at both the meeting level and per-speaker level.
Finally, the Action Plan Generation module extracts tasks, identifies the responsible participant, and detects deadlines — outputting a structured action plan ready for immediate use.
The entire architecture is modular and supports both cloud and local deployment for privacy-sensitive environments.

Slide 7 — System Workflow (30 seconds)
The end-to-end workflow follows a clean pipeline — starting from audio capture, through speech-to-text conversion and speaker labeling, into NLP-based topic and intent analysis, action item detection, summary generation, and finally producing the structured action plan. Each stage feeds the next, making the system both coherent and extensible.

Slide 8 — Dataset (25 seconds)
We used two datasets. The Meeting Audio and Transcript Dataset captures features like audio file, speaker ID, timestamp, language, and ground truth transcription — used to train our ASR and summarization modules.
The Action Annotation Dataset annotates utterances with action type, assigned participant, deadline, and confidence score — used to train the action extraction model.
The annotated data was split 80:20 for training and testing.

Slide 9 — Model Architecture & Implementation (30 seconds)
The core implementation uses a hybrid architecture. ASR models handle audio-to-text conversion. Transformer-based NLP models handle summarization and semantic understanding. A supervised action classification model detects tasks, owners, and deadlines from the processed transcript.
The web application was built using Python Flask. Each extracted action item carries a confidence score, and a feedback mechanism allows the system to iteratively improve accuracy over time.

Slide 10 — Results & Discussion (50 seconds)
Our evaluation covered three dimensions.
For transcription accuracy, the system achieved an average Word Error Rate of just 6.85%, corresponding to about 93% accuracy — competitive with state-of-the-art ASR systems on meeting-domain audio.
For summarization quality, we measured ROUGE scores: ROUGE-1 of 0.82, ROUGE-2 of 0.74, and ROUGE-L of 0.80 — indicating strong overlap between generated and reference summaries.
For action extraction, the system achieved a Precision of 0.88, Recall of 0.86, and an F1-score of 0.87. Owner attribution accuracy was 91% and deadline detection accuracy was 84%.
And in our user study, task identification time was reduced by 65% compared to manual transcript review. 87% of users reported improved clarity on responsibilities, and 91% agreed it reduced post-meeting cognitive load. You can see the extracted action items table here — tasks, owners, and deadlines, all automatically generated.

Slide 11 — Conclusion & Future Work (25 seconds)
To summarize — we presented the Meeting Summarizer, an end-to-end AI system that converts unstructured meeting transcripts into structured, accountable action plans. It integrates STT, transformer summarization, and supervised action extraction in a single modular pipeline, with strong quantitative results and validated usability improvements.
For future work, we plan to expand multilingual datasets, conduct large-scale real-world validation, develop personalized adaptive summarization, and integrate with productivity platforms like Microsoft Teams and Slack.

